da }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186305096 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534919 17592186045417 false] [17592186391144 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186391144 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139534919 17592186045417 false] [17592186444119 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444150 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444151 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444152 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444153 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444154 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1045 "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1046 #edb/ref 17592186444153 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048395 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444119 1053 #uuid "9a145625-7556-5712-927a-f0c028bebc85" 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444120 1080 "an unstable node (source)" 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444120 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444120 1083 #uuid "1d7975e3-2735-589b-a5cb-e7baa70e5b2a" 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444121 1080 "a stable node (sink)" 13194139534919 17592186045417 true] [17592186444121 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534919 17592186